WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.791
Production Blazor applications need secure
configuration,

2
00:00:06.871 --> 00:00:09.895
not just working components.

3
00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:17.476
Hosting model, render mode, API endpoints,
identity provider settings,

4
00:00:17.556 --> 00:00:21.427
logging sinks, dependency versions,

5
00:00:21.507 --> 00:00:27.072
and environment flags all shape production
behavior.

6
00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:35.137
A component that works locally still needs
deployment review before release.

7
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:42.597
Secrets should not be embedded in
client-side code, downloaded assets,

8
00:00:42.677 --> 00:00:48.605
configuration shipped to the browser, or
source control.

9
00:00:48.685 --> 00:00:57.274
This is especially important for
WebAssembly and other browser-delivered assets.

10
00:00:57.354 --> 00:01:01.226
If a value is delivered to the browser,

11
00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:06.750
it should not be treated as a protected
server secret.

12
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:14.209
Development settings should not silently
become production behavior.

13
00:01:14.289 --> 00:01:21.427
Detailed errors, permissive origins,
temporary identity settings,

14
00:01:21.507 --> 00:01:25.137
broad logging, preview endpoints,

15
00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:31.508
and test service endpoints can be useful
during development.

16
00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:39.693
They need explicit environment controls
and release review before production

17
00:01:39.773 --> 00:01:41.104
deployment.

18
00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:48.322
Useful logs include authentication events,
authorization denials,

19
00:01:48.402 --> 00:01:56.386
validation failures, API errors,
unexpected exceptions, upload anomalies,

20
00:01:56.466 --> 00:01:59.491
and administrative changes.

21
00:01:59.571 --> 00:02:07.797
Logs should support operations without
recording passwords, tokens, secrets,

22
00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:15.257
raw sensitive request content, stack
traces in client-visible places,

23
00:02:15.337 --> 00:02:21.386
connection strings, or internal
implementation details.

24
00:02:21.466 --> 00:02:27.394
Production readiness includes HTTPS,
security headers,

25
00:02:27.474 --> 00:02:35.217
CORS and CSRF review, dependency review,
environment review, monitoring,

26
00:02:35.297 --> 00:02:40.741
backup and restore awareness, and incident
handoff.

27
00:02:40.821 --> 00:02:46.386
These controls are not separate from
Blazor security.

28
00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:54.571
They determine how the application behaves
when it is operated by real teams and

29
00:02:54.651 --> 00:02:56.587
used by real users.

30
00:02:56.667 --> 00:03:04.652
A strong readiness review asks where
secrets live, what the browser receives,

31
00:03:04.732 --> 00:03:10.539
which APIs are exposed, which dependencies
are shipped,

32
00:03:10.619 --> 00:03:16.668
what errors users can see, what operators
can investigate,

33
00:03:16.748 --> 00:03:20.740
and who owns exceptions after release.
